  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Given that \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU$, then ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=$",
  "worked_solution": "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1}\\,\u27fa\\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{\u23de}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]}_{\u23df}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-1,{u}_{22}=0,$ and ${l}_{21}+{u}_{22}=-1.$",
